provider_responses.text:
A team is trying to eliminate an unwanted behavior in a software product: users repeatedly take an old, unhelpful route through the interface.

The team’s goal should not be framed as:

> “Delete the old route from existence.”

Instead, the practical goal is:

> “Make the preferred route so clear, fast, reliable, and available in every realistic usage condition that people no longer choose the old one.”

That requires more than one redesign. It involves:

- removing cues that keep directing users toward the old flow;
- making the new flow simpler and faster;
- testing it when users are rushed, distracted, tired, or under pressure;
- reducing the perceived value of the old option;
- measuring actual behavior rather than assuming the old pattern is gone.

The risk is pursuing an extreme redesign that overwhelms users or damages other parts of the product. If every feature is changed at once, users may become confused, adoption may collapse, and the team may lose sight of what actually improved behavior.

So the core problem is to balance ambition with reality:

- aim for the old flow to become practically irrelevant;
- do not assume it can be made literally impossible in every edge case;
- define success with observable metrics, such as reduced use, faster completion, fewer errors, and stable performance under difficult conditions;
- improve iteratively rather than treating intensity alone as proof of progress.

