provider_responses.text:
The most important part is this:

> Make the preferred route so clear, fast, reliable, and available under real conditions that people stop choosing the old route on their own.

A product cannot rely on users having perfect attention, motivation, memory, or patience. People use software while distracted, rushed, tired, confused, emotionally activated, on poor connections, or under social and work pressure. If the new route only works for a calm, focused, highly motivated user, it has not replaced the old route in practice.

The preferred behavior must win in the actual moment of choice.

That means designing for four things.

### 1. Lower friction

The desired action should require fewer steps, less interpretation, and less effort than the old action.

For example:

- put the right action where the user already is;
- use language that makes the next step obvious;
- eliminate unnecessary decisions;
- preserve entered data and context;
- make recovery from mistakes easy;
- avoid requiring users to remember instructions from earlier screens.

If the old route feels familiar but the new route feels complicated, people will revert under pressure—even if they agree the new route is objectively better.

### 2. Stronger cues

Users often follow what the interface makes most visible, familiar, and immediate.

The product should clearly signal:

- what is happening;
- what action is expected now;
- why that action is useful;
- what will happen next.

The preferred route should not merely be available somewhere in the product. It should be the route naturally suggested by the layout, wording, defaults, timing, and feedback.

A hidden “better option” is not behavior change. It is documentation.

### 3. Reliability in difficult conditions

The route must remain usable when reality is messy:

- slow internet;
- mobile screens;
- interrupted sessions;
- incomplete information;
- accessibility needs;
- repeated use;
- high workload;
- unfamiliar users;
- urgent situations.

If the new flow fails or becomes slow in those moments, users will learn that the old method is safer. One highly visible failure can outweigh many smooth demonstrations in ideal conditions.

This is why testing only with clean prototypes and attentive participants is insufficient. The question is not just, “Can users complete this?” It is:

> “Will they still choose this when doing so matters and conditions are bad?”

### 4. Devalue the old route without trapping people

The old route may persist because it is familiar, appears safer, gives users more perceived control, or solves edge cases the new route does not yet handle.

The team should understand why people keep using it before trying to suppress it. Then reduce its attractiveness by making the preferred route genuinely better:

- faster results;
- clearer outcomes;
- fewer errors;
- better recovery;
- less manual work;
- more trustworthy feedback.

Do not simply hide or remove the old route before the replacement can handle what users need. That may create frustration, workarounds, support burden, or loss of trust.

The ideal outcome is not that users feel forced away from the old route. It is that they no longer see a reason to use it.

### A practical definition of success

Success is not:

> “The old behavior never occurs again.”

A better operational definition is:

- the preferred route is selected by default in normal use;
- it remains the most common choice under pressure and edge cases;
- users complete their task faster and with fewer errors;
- users do not need reminders, training, or workarounds to follow it;
- use of the old route becomes rare, explainable, and limited to legitimate exceptions.

In product terms, the desired flow has become the dominant path—not because people were ordered to use it, but because the product consistently makes it the easiest and most dependable way forward.

